# Ben-Or, Kelmer, Rabin 1994
# "Asynchronous Secure Computations with Optimal Resilience"
# Section 4 Figure 3 - Protocol Agreement[Q]
#
# Paper-faithful capture of the BKR94 Asynchronous Common Subset
# (ACS) protocol's rules.  Identifier and predicate translation,
# and any C-side bridge decisions (sender validation, message
# routing across process/round/initiator, etc.), live in
# bkr94acsToC.dtc.
#
# Composition.  BKR94 ACS is built on top of N reliable-broadcast
# instances (one per process A-Casting a value) and N binary-
# agreement instances BA_j (one per process, deciding whether to
# include that process's A-Cast in the agreed subset).  The
# rules below describe what the ACS layer does in response to
# the events those underlying primitives surface; the primitives
# themselves are specified in bracha87Fig1.dtc (A-Cast) and
# bracha87Fig3.dtc / bracha87Fig4.dtc (BA).
#
# In this deployment Q(j) = 1 means P_j has broadcast a value,
# realized by Bracha87's Figure 1 accepting the reliable
# broadcast of P_j's A-Cast.  Bracha's Lemma 4 makes this
# realization satisfy BKR94's assumption (2) on Q (if any honest
# accepts, every honest eventually accepts).  Q is application-
# supplied and BKR94's own MPC construction instantiated it
# differently, so this choice is recorded in bkr94acsToC.dtc and
# is not a paper concern.
#
# The Step 2 trigger is "n-t BA outputs of 1," not "n-t Q(j) = 1
# events".  These coincide in benign runs but diverge under
# asynchrony or Byzantine scheduling; only the BA-output-1
# trigger satisfies Part A case (i) of the BKR94 Lemma 2 proof.
# An optimization seen in derived presentations, which fires Step 2
# on the count of Q(j) = 1 events, would be the wrong reading of
# the paper.
#
# Step 2, like every rule in the paper, is stated in the
# asynchronous model: unbounded (finite) message delay, no clocks
# (BKR94ACS.txt, Model and tools).  Each rule's "upon" names the
# evidence that enables an action; the model has no notion of the
# moment the action is taken, and Lemma 2 Part B consumes only that
# the 0-inputs are eventually entered once the n-t count holds.  An
# earlier revision of this table encoded Step 2 on the arrival
# path, firing at the instant the local count crossed n-t.  That
# eager schedule was this capture's reading, not the paper's -- it
# dropped the unbounded-latency notion the model rests on.  The
# Step 2 section below records the rule; its firing is paced by the
# caller from the BPR sweep tick, completing for this layer the BPR
# integration bracha87.[hc] already has.  A zero tolerance budget
# recovers the eager schedule.
#
# Inputs (the situation when an ACS-layer event is processed;
# j is the process to which the event refers):
#   "kind of ACS event"     { Q(j) = 1, BA_j outputs 0, BA_j outputs 1 }
#   "input to BA_j"         { none, 0, 1 }
#                             - the value, if any, this process
#                               has previously entered to BA_j
#   "post-output BA-output count == n"               { yes, no }
#                             - the total number of BAs that
#                               have output any value, after
#                               this event is recorded
#
# Outputs:
#   "input 1 to BA_j"               { yes, no }
#   "output SubSet"                 { yes, no }
#                                     - SubSet := { j : BA_j had output 1 }
#                                       computed when this output fires

# ================================================================
# Step 1 - Input 1 to BA_j when Q(j) = 1
# ================================================================

# Rule 1.  When Q(j) = 1 becomes known to me, if I have not
# entered a value to BA_j, input 1 to BA_j.  BKR94 Lemma 4 (via
# the underlying reliable broadcast) guarantees that every
# honest process eventually learns Q(j) = 1 if any does, so
# every honest process eventually executes Rule 1 for j.
@"input 1 to BA_j","kind of ACS event","input to BA_j"
yes,"Q(j) = 1",none
no,"Q(j) = 1",0
no,"Q(j) = 1",1
no,"BA_j outputs 0",
no,"BA_j outputs 1",

# ================================================================
# Step 2 - Input 0 to all unentered BAs at n-t BA outputs of 1
# ================================================================

# Rule 2 -- recorded here, fired from the BPR sweep.
#
# The paper's rule: "Upon completing 2t+1 BA protocols with output
# 1, enter input 0 to every BA protocol for which you have not yet
# entered a value."
#
# Enabling evidence, not a moment (header note above).  Fired at
# the instant the count crosses -- the earlier revision of this
# table -- the fastest n-t processes close SubSet against every
# honest process whose A-Cast is still in flight, and under a
# persistent latency spread the same honest processes are excluded
# every ACS instance.  Nothing in the model compels that
# eagerness; the deployment's answer to unbounded latency is the
# BPR sweep, so the firing lives there, beside the retry/retire
# decisions that already do.
#
# bkr94acsFanoutDuty classifies the decision, derived by scanning
# baDecision[] and entered[] (never stored):
#
#   HELD      - BA-output-1 count < n-t.  The paper's floor: enter
#               0 below it and a mass of 0-inputs could force
#               SubSet empty.  Elapsed tolerance is irrelevant.
#   TOLERANCE - count >= n-t and unentered BAs remain.  Enabled.
#               The caller counts sweeps against its budget and
#               calls bkr94acsFanout when the budget elapses; a
#               zero budget is the eager schedule.  While TOLERANCE
#               holds, the same sweep keeps retrying the delayed
#               processes' A-Casts (BPR gate below: undecided ->
#               retry), so the grace is spent on exactly the
#               recovery work that can make the firing unnecessary.
#   MET       - no unentered BAs remain; nothing to give up on.
#               Absorbing: the fanout empties the unentered set, so
#               once-per-instance is structural and the old "fanout
#               already triggered" latch (BKR94ACS_F_THRESHOLD) is
#               gone.
#
# The trichotomy names the one decision shape -- proceed without
# the tail -- under the caller discipline both duty queries share
# (count sweeps only while TOLERANCE; the budget is deployment
# tuning).
#
# Out of the merged dispatch because its inputs (the n-t count,
# the unentered set, elapsed tolerance) appear in no other rule --
# merging would duplicate its DAG at every leaf -- and tolerance
# is deployment vocabulary, not paper vocabulary.  The enabling
# predicate and the fanout loop (enter 0 where input is none) are
# plain C in bkr94acsFanoutDuty / bkr94acsFanout, the same
# treatment as the BPR retry gate below.

# ================================================================
# The BA round turn -- not a BKR94 rule; fired from the BPR sweep
# ================================================================

# The composition's other sweep-side decision.  Bracha's Fig4 steps
# read "Wait until validate n-t k-messages" and then compute over
# the validated set (the majority at 3i+1, the decide/adopt
# thresholds at 3i+3).  The set keeps growing past n-t and Bracha's
# proofs hold for ANY >= n-t sample -- so the sample a turn
# consumes is purely a function of WHEN the turn fires.  An earlier
# revision turned rounds inside the arrival path (bkr94acsBaInput's
# cascade loop), consuming the first n-t; the same eager-schedule
# reading Step 2 carried, with a different cost: not exclusion (a
# late round-k message still validates and still serves round-k+1
# validation) but coin luck.  At 3i+3 the decide/adopt counts are
# monotone thresholds (per-sender dedup: counts only grow), so a
# fuller sample can only convert coin phases into deterministic
# decides; at 3i+1 the majority is a comparison a fuller sample can
# flip -- both outcomes proof-covered, the flip trading between
# sound broadcasts, never against safety.
#
# The arrival path now only BANKS evidence (Fig1 input, Fig3
# store/validate/cascade -- Fig3 itself was always deferral-shaped:
# every message is stored and validity re-derived as prior rounds
# grow).  bkr94acsTurnDuty classifies a BA's next round with the
# shared trichotomy (HELD below n-t validated or round space
# exhausted / TOLERANCE at >= n-t with the sample still growable /
# MET at all n validated -- the full sample, firing is free), and
# bkr94acsTurn computes one round under the same caller discipline
# as the fanout.  BA_DECIDED / COMPLETE / BA_EXHAUSTED emerge from
# turns only; the Step 3 dispatch below rides the turn's decide.
#
# bracha87.[hc] needed no change: bracha87Fig3RoundComplete,
# bracha87Fig3GetValid, and bracha87Fig4Round were already the
# caller-shaped seam; the eager loop was this composition's choice.

# ================================================================
# Step 3 - Output SubSet when all n BAs have output
# ================================================================

# Rule 3.  Upon any BA_j outputting, if all n BAs have now
# output a value, output SubSet := { j : BA_j had output 1 }.
# BKR94 Lemma 2 Part A guarantees |SubSet| >= 2t+1 = n-t; Part C
# guarantees cross-process agreement on SubSet; Part D
# guarantees Q(j) = 1 for every j in SubSet.  The "all n BAs"
# threshold can only be reached once per ACS instance (each BA
# outputs at most once), so this rule is naturally idempotent.
#
# Computing the SubSet itself - iterating over the N BAs and
# collecting indices with output 1 - is a C-side loop; the DTC
# fires the go-signal and the C produces the set.
@"output SubSet","kind of ACS event","post-output BA-output count == n"
yes,"BA_j outputs 1",yes
yes,"BA_j outputs 0",yes
no,"BA_j outputs 1",no
no,"BA_j outputs 0",no
no,"Q(j) = 1",

# Bracha Phase Retry (BPR) per-process retry gate -- not in
# this dispatch.
#
# Justification for BPR's placement at this layer: end-to-end
# argument (Saltzer/Reed/Clark 1984; see SRC84.txt).  The
# "still owed" predicate for A-Cast-Fig1 retry combines
# Bracha's sent flags with this layer's per-process
# BA-decided state (undecided / decided 0 / decided 1); both
# live at the BKR endpoint, so the retire-or-retry decision is
# made here rather than below.
#
# bkr94acsRetry walks per-process A-Cast Fig1 instances and
# decides whether to output retries based on the local BA
# decision state ({ undecided, decided 0, decided 1 }):
#   - undecided  -> retry  (Q(j)=1 may still be learned by
#                          other honest processes; their Fig1(j)
#                          needs our echoes / readys)
#   - decided 0  -> skip  (j excluded from SubSet; Step 2's
#                          fanout already conveyed our enter)
#   - decided 1  -> retry  (Bracha post-decide continuation:
#                          processes that have not yet observed
#                          Fig1 ACCEPT for j still need our
#                          echoes / readys, else their BA_j
#                          stays undecided and may go to 0
#                          via Step 2, violating cross-process
#                          agreement on SubSet)
#
# Three-rule single-input single-output guard with no
# interaction with the Rules 1-3 dispatch and no test ordering
# to optimize; captured in C as a switch over the BA decision
# byte rather than a sub-table (same call as the Fig1 initiator
# INITIAL retry gate -- see bracha87Fig1.dtc BPR section).
# The pitfall #1 (post-decide continuation) reasoning lives in
# the comment at the bkr94acsRetry cursor site.
